WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.492 --> 00:00:03.671
- He's a Swedish video game
programmer and designer.

00:00:03.671 --> 00:00:05.606
He co-founded the video game company

00:00:05.606 --> 00:00:08.208
that created Minecraft in 2010.

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:12.327
He has an estimated net
worth of $1.5 billion.

00:00:12.327 --> 00:00:13.996
He is Markus Notch Persson,

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:17.496
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:19.942 --> 00:00:21.116
- [Interviewer] Can you
tell us a little bit

00:00:21.116 --> 00:00:23.787
about how you got started
in video game development?

00:00:23.787 --> 00:00:25.620
- Oh, well, when I was really,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.368
really young, like eight years old,

00:00:27.368 --> 00:00:30.941
my dad got us a Commodore 128
and we started subscribing

00:00:30.941 --> 00:00:33.648
to a magazine that had like
code listings for games.

00:00:33.648 --> 00:00:36.172
And I started entering them
and started toying with them.

00:00:36.172 --> 00:00:38.031
It's been with me for a long time.

00:00:38.031 --> 00:00:39.487
And it's kind of gradually growing,

00:00:39.487 --> 00:00:41.490
then it became a profession.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:45.120
Then I quit that job and just went Indie.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.898
- [Interviewer] And what
advice would you give to people

00:00:46.898 --> 00:00:50.158
who want to get started in video
game development themselves?

00:00:50.158 --> 00:00:52.240
- Well, yeah, look at what other

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.302
people have done, look at tutorials,

00:00:54.302 --> 00:00:56.672
look at code and just play with the code.

00:00:56.672 --> 00:00:57.759
Then once you're comfortable with it,

00:00:57.759 --> 00:00:59.744
don't try to make your biggest game.

00:00:59.744 --> 00:01:02.309
Just try to make a small
game and actually finish it.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:03.899
I've been playing games forever

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:05.130
and I've been programming
since I was eight.

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.147
So it's obvious I have some kind

00:01:06.147 --> 00:01:08.455
of experience in it and I kind of,

00:01:08.455 --> 00:01:10.237
I can understand kind
of what's fun gameplay

00:01:10.237 --> 00:01:12.839
but I didn't mean to
make like a big success.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:16.352
To claim that I know the secret
sauce would be kind of false.

00:01:16.352 --> 00:01:17.700
- There's no formula.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.979
- I wasn't following a formula,

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:20.855
I was just trying to make a game.

00:01:20.855 --> 00:01:23.832
Well, I think actually working on it

00:01:23.832 --> 00:01:25.736
is more important than thinking about it.

00:01:25.736 --> 00:01:27.388
So if you just get
started, even if it's not

00:01:27.388 --> 00:01:29.413
something big, even if it's just small,

00:01:29.413 --> 00:01:32.075
just try to make games
or look at tutorials

00:01:32.075 --> 00:01:34.656
or play with the code other
people have made or something.

00:01:34.656 --> 00:01:36.383
And then also to keep a critical eye

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:37.899
towards what you're doing.

00:01:37.899 --> 00:01:40.464
'Cause it is very easy to just assume

00:01:40.464 --> 00:01:43.602
what you are doing is good
because you're used to it.

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:45.396
But if you're just trying
to see it with like the eyes

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:47.485
of a new player helps a lot.

00:01:47.485 --> 00:01:49.639
I think that the best advice
that I could give everyone is

00:01:49.639 --> 00:01:51.114
don't listen to advice.

00:01:51.114 --> 00:01:52.834
But if you are going to listen to advice

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:54.399
then making sure you actually

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:56.077
do something is a good advice.

00:01:56.077 --> 00:01:58.379
If you just around planning
stuff and you're not doing it,

00:01:58.379 --> 00:02:00.394
you're not actually going to get anywhere.

00:02:00.394 --> 00:02:03.780
- You sold your company
recently to Microsoft.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.874
But you haven't really
been talking about it.

00:02:06.874 --> 00:02:08.354
So, what happened there?

00:02:08.354 --> 00:02:09.672
- I wasn't working on Minecraft anymore

00:02:09.672 --> 00:02:10.758
and I hadn't been for a long time.

00:02:10.758 --> 00:02:12.600
But I still kind of was responsible for it

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.426
as a main, I was the main
shareholder of Mojang.

00:02:16.426 --> 00:02:19.574
And then this big thing
happened with EULA and stuff,

00:02:19.574 --> 00:02:22.681
people thought we changed
the rules and like.

00:02:22.681 --> 00:02:24.262
- [Interviewer] The end
user licencee agreement.

00:02:24.262 --> 00:02:26.117
- Yeah, which had always been in place,

00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:27.808
but what actually happened
is that some people brought

00:02:27.808 --> 00:02:29.952
it up like so we're not
allowed to do this and that.

00:02:29.952 --> 00:02:31.931
And we was like no, so we
looked it over and actually

00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:33.802
made the rules more lenient.

00:02:33.802 --> 00:02:34.635
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:02:34.635 --> 00:02:37.425
- But people still got very
upset with like me, personally,

00:02:37.425 --> 00:02:40.388
even though I wasn't even
working over there more.

00:02:40.388 --> 00:02:43.383
I don't know if I want to
put up with this any more.

00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:44.877
And then I saw the This
is Phil Fish video,

00:02:44.877 --> 00:02:47.268
this is a YouTube documentary
about a guy called

00:02:47.268 --> 00:02:51.266
Phil Fish and he talks about
how like internet personalities

00:02:51.266 --> 00:02:54.449
and like famous people aren't really,

00:02:54.449 --> 00:02:55.911
they're not talking to that actual person

00:02:55.911 --> 00:02:56.821
when they're talking to a person,

00:02:56.821 --> 00:02:59.248
they're talking about
that idea of the person.

00:02:59.248 --> 00:03:01.683
How does this connect when
you respond personally

00:03:01.683 --> 00:03:04.278
to stuff that's not really
intended to be personal.

00:03:04.278 --> 00:03:06.444
And I kind of just
figured like I'd probably,

00:03:06.444 --> 00:03:08.493
maybe I don't have to put up with this.

00:03:08.493 --> 00:03:10.746
So yes, when that happened, and everyone

00:03:10.746 --> 00:03:12.732
just giving so much like crap online,

00:03:12.732 --> 00:03:14.695
I just went no, it's
not worth it any more.

00:03:14.695 --> 00:03:15.528
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.063
- So I just tweeted kind of jokingly,

00:03:18.063 --> 00:03:20.476
who wants to buy the company?

00:03:20.476 --> 00:03:23.275
And a couple of days
later, Carl the CEO came by

00:03:23.275 --> 00:03:27.322
and said he got a bunch of
offers, like were you serious?

00:03:27.322 --> 00:03:28.328
And I thought about if for a while

00:03:28.328 --> 00:03:30.465
and I said yeah, maybe it's time.

00:03:30.465 --> 00:03:32.211
- How do you run your Minecraft company?

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:34.644
Have you sold out the
evil corporations yet?

00:03:34.644 --> 00:03:36.666
- No, not yet, we hope not to.

00:03:36.666 --> 00:03:37.901
- How long you think
you'll be able to do that

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:41.341
before you kind of get
absorbed by the borg?

00:03:41.341 --> 00:03:43.484
(audience laughing)

00:03:43.484 --> 00:03:45.411
- I think that depends on
how long we can kind of keep

00:03:45.411 --> 00:03:47.274
the fun mood going that we have.

00:03:47.274 --> 00:03:49.391
That's very stressful
and it really got to me.

00:03:49.391 --> 00:03:51.071
I don't want to work
on the product any more

00:03:51.071 --> 00:03:53.613
'cause I wanted to just write
prototypes for new games

00:03:53.613 --> 00:03:54.834
and stuff that just,

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:57.043
the stuff I actually get happy from doing.

00:03:57.043 --> 00:03:58.370
And I felt like, still like,

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.556
I needed to be responsible for it.

00:03:59.556 --> 00:04:01.953
'Cause I was still like the
main owner of the company

00:04:01.953 --> 00:04:03.800
and it was me who developed
the game and people

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.653
were like looking up to me.

00:04:05.653 --> 00:04:07.105
And for me it was very
important to make sure

00:04:07.105 --> 00:04:08.816
that it was like a clean break.

00:04:08.816 --> 00:04:11.959
Like it wasn't like trying
to get some investor in

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:14.254
and try to grow the
company, do like a big exit.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.490
It was more about getting it quick

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.538
and easy, and just get it done with.

00:04:17.538 --> 00:04:20.982
I think the difference there
is kind of people do something

00:04:20.982 --> 00:04:23.575
as their job and as their hobby,

00:04:23.575 --> 00:04:25.955
you get so much more
experience so much faster.

00:04:25.955 --> 00:04:30.077
So you can kind of skip a
few of the steps I think.

00:04:30.077 --> 00:04:32.196
I think it's very important to know

00:04:32.196 --> 00:04:34.145
you're not going to be an
expert right off the bat,

00:04:34.145 --> 00:04:35.394
you have to, 'cause I've been programming

00:04:35.394 --> 00:04:39.214
since I was eight and I'm
far from an expert still.

00:04:39.214 --> 00:04:41.684
It's like you can't expect the first

00:04:41.684 --> 00:04:42.778
thing you do to be a hit.

00:04:42.778 --> 00:04:44.994
So having a few jobs and
such is probably a good idea.

00:04:44.994 --> 00:04:47.715
Before King I didn't
know how to finish games

00:04:47.715 --> 00:04:51.184
so that was a huge experience for me.

00:04:51.184 --> 00:04:52.437
Yeah, but it isn't easy as a hobby

00:04:52.437 --> 00:04:55.120
the way I like and I got to learn faster

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.924
than just doing it as a job.

00:04:56.924 --> 00:05:00.564
Yeah, letting other people
into the code was really scary.

00:05:00.564 --> 00:05:03.975
Jess's the guy now,
working Minecraft with me.

00:05:03.975 --> 00:05:05.110
- One change I've made is that

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.142
spiders will climb up walls.

00:05:08.142 --> 00:05:09.285
- [Markus] You used the ladder thing?

00:05:09.285 --> 00:05:10.118
- Yeah.

00:05:10.118 --> 00:05:10.951
- [Markus] Okay.

00:05:10.951 --> 00:05:11.867
- They are always on ladders.

00:05:11.867 --> 00:05:13.773
(laughing)

00:05:13.773 --> 00:05:17.169
So if they, okay yeah, that's
so freaking clever hack.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.199
- Every time you change stuff
somebody is going to say

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:24.048
that it's better for it,
they're going to say ah,

00:05:24.048 --> 00:05:27.630
it's much better but most
of the time you have to

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.157
like focus on what is real
part of feeling yourself,

00:05:30.157 --> 00:05:32.117
if it's good or bad.

00:05:32.117 --> 00:05:36.215
Another thing, a suggestion
is to make a scarecrow

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:39.188
because it would be so fun
to have like a pumpkin here,

00:05:39.188 --> 00:05:41.916
and then some cloth and then some sticks.

00:05:41.916 --> 00:05:43.345
It would look very cute.

00:05:43.345 --> 00:05:45.143
- Or the hay, in the middle.

00:05:45.143 --> 00:05:46.718
- Ah, yeah the hay.

00:05:46.718 --> 00:05:48.903
- Maybe if you used like a
target dummy for the creepers.

00:05:48.903 --> 00:05:50.510
- Yeah maybe it would attract monsters.

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:51.519
- [Markus] Yes, and we should

00:05:51.519 --> 00:05:53.664
have snowmen as well that
could throw snowballs.

00:05:53.664 --> 00:05:55.793
(laughing)

00:05:55.793 --> 00:05:59.467
It's like, when I see
suggestions from the community,

00:05:59.467 --> 00:06:00.969
I try not add it because it feels like

00:06:00.969 --> 00:06:03.589
I'm doing like, just
they just ask for things

00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:05.263
and I add them and that's not necessarily

00:06:05.263 --> 00:06:08.479
my idea that's driving it any more.

00:06:08.479 --> 00:06:10.302
But that's changing now because

00:06:10.302 --> 00:06:12.909
Jess is adding stuff that he thinks works.

00:06:12.909 --> 00:06:16.409
But when I see it actually in
the game and it makes sense,

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:19.795
I can just much easier accept it.

00:06:19.795 --> 00:06:22.701
It worked a lot better
than I thought it would.

00:06:22.701 --> 00:06:26.297
I think it's a matter of
just letting go, I think.

00:06:26.297 --> 00:06:28.076
'Cause it's good for the game.

00:06:28.076 --> 00:06:30.995
The trap look was just brilliant.

00:06:30.995 --> 00:06:35.284
- Yeah right, now it's really
fun to add stuff to Minecraft.

00:06:35.284 --> 00:06:38.784
It's also fun to be part of this so early,

00:06:40.096 --> 00:06:42.846
and really be with the core team.

00:06:43.727 --> 00:06:47.007
I don't know how big we're
going to be in the future,

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:50.157
but it's fun to be here
from almost the beginning.

00:06:50.157 --> 00:06:52.164
- I have been working on like prototypes

00:06:52.164 --> 00:06:53.766
and trying to learn new languages.

00:06:53.766 --> 00:06:55.400
But it is not really the intent

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.038
that I'm going to finish a game.

00:06:57.038 --> 00:06:58.983
Maybe I will at some point,
and then it is good to have

00:06:58.983 --> 00:07:00.574
like company set up for it.

00:07:00.574 --> 00:07:02.619
And it's fun to have like
the idea when you work

00:07:02.619 --> 00:07:05.148
and stuff like it's supposed
to become something.

00:07:05.148 --> 00:07:07.107
But not feel pressure like it has to.

00:07:07.107 --> 00:07:09.530
- So you could possibly
never release a game again.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:11.365
- Yeah, and I would be fine with that.

00:07:11.365 --> 00:07:12.903
It was a long time
where I was freaking out

00:07:12.903 --> 00:07:14.934
like how do I follow up on Minecraft,

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:17.375
and it was really hard to deal with.

00:07:17.375 --> 00:07:20.344
'Cause it's this entire
big cultural phenomenon.

00:07:20.344 --> 00:07:22.143
You can't manufacture that.

00:07:22.143 --> 00:07:23.441
Maybe now that I've analyzed it

00:07:23.441 --> 00:07:25.945
I understand some of why Minecraft worked,

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:29.362
but that only means I
know how Minecraft worked

00:07:29.362 --> 00:07:31.345
back in time in the
context it was made in.

00:07:31.345 --> 00:07:32.178
- Right.

00:07:32.178 --> 00:07:33.458
- So that doesn't mean I
know how to do it again.

00:07:33.458 --> 00:07:36.857
I kind of feel like I made a
game, and then the community

00:07:36.857 --> 00:07:38.967
kind of made the
community around the game.

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:42.928
And the combination of the two
is kind of that true Minecraft.

00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:47.030
So it's way more than just
the game I made about just,

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:49.289
chopping rock kind of slowly.

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:51.432
For example, creative mode is not what

00:07:51.432 --> 00:07:53.109
I wanted in the game at
all, but it's in the game

00:07:53.109 --> 00:07:54.617
because a lot of people use it.

00:07:54.617 --> 00:07:56.594
And then the people doing really cool mods

00:07:56.594 --> 00:07:58.938
and they're doing really intricate stuff

00:07:58.938 --> 00:08:03.566
like abusing bugs and
redstones so we can't fix it,

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:05.688
it's turned into something much bigger

00:08:05.688 --> 00:08:07.843
and it's very impressive,
and it's very fun

00:08:07.843 --> 00:08:09.938
to be able to be a part of that.

00:08:09.938 --> 00:08:11.475
I think when I was like really young

00:08:11.475 --> 00:08:13.703
and said I wanted to
be a cop or something.

00:08:13.703 --> 00:08:16.288
But I kind of always knew that I wanted

00:08:16.288 --> 00:08:19.871
to make games and I
said so in school to my

00:08:21.104 --> 00:08:23.476
career counselor thing, and she said

00:08:23.476 --> 00:08:24.892
well, that's probably not going to happen.

00:08:24.892 --> 00:08:26.413
- [Interviewer] Mhm.

00:08:26.413 --> 00:08:27.246
(laughing)

00:08:27.246 --> 00:08:28.079
- Yeah.

00:08:30.214 --> 00:08:31.553
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:08:31.553 --> 00:08:34.315
I made this video because Zed Schee

00:08:34.315 --> 00:08:36.024
asked me too, so if there's a famous

00:08:36.024 --> 00:08:38.703
entrepreneur that you
want me to profile next,

00:08:38.703 --> 00:08:41.344
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:08:41.344 --> 00:08:43.571
I'd also love to know
which of the top 10 rules

00:08:43.571 --> 00:08:45.367
had the biggest impact on you.

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.948
Leave it in the comments, and
I will join in the discussion.

00:08:47.948 --> 00:08:49.130
Thank you so much for watching,

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.713
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

